  "content": [
    {
      "id": "introduction",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Before getting into DP, let's understand&nbsp;<strong>what a bitmask actually is</strong>&nbsp;and why it's useful.",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "1-what-is-a-bitmask",
      "title": "1. What is a Bitmask?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A bitmask is just an integer. But instead of treating it as a number, you treat each&nbsp;<strong>bit as a switch</strong>&nbsp;on or off, true or false, included or not.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197643-876087485.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197643-876087485.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197643-876087485.png",
          "alt": "What is a Bitmask?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Number 6 in binary is&nbsp;<code>110</code>, where each bit represents whether an item is included or not.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>Reading from right to left:</b><br>bit0 = 0 → item 0 is not included<br>bit1 = 1 → item 1 is included<br>bit2 = 1 → item 2 is included",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "So mask&nbsp;<code>110</code>&nbsp;means items 1 and 2 are selected, and item 0 is not.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "So the integer&nbsp;<code>6</code>&nbsp;represents the set&nbsp;<code>{item1, item2}</code>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "With just one integer, you can represent&nbsp;<strong>which items from a set are selected</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "<hr style=\"border-width: 1px medium medium; border-style: solid none none; border-color: rgb(224, 224, 224) currentcolor currentcolor; border-image: none; margin: 24px 0px;\">",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "2-why-bitmasks-for-dp",
      "title": "2. Why Bitmasks for DP?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Some problems require you to track&nbsp;<strong>which items have been visited or used</strong>. Normally you'd use a boolean array for that:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "visited = [true, false, true, true, false]"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The problem you can't use a boolean array as a DP key directly. You'd need a separate state for every possible combination of visited items.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "With a bitmask the entire visited state collapses into&nbsp;<strong>one integer</strong>:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197643-650130575.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197643-650130575.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197643-650130575.png",
          "alt": "Why Bitmasks for DP?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The visited array&nbsp;<code>[true, false, true, true, false]</code>&nbsp;is converted into binary by treating&nbsp;<code>true = 1</code>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<code>false = 0</code>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "So it becomes&nbsp;<code>1 0 1 1 0</code>, which is&nbsp;<code>10110</code>&nbsp;in binary.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This binary number represents the visited state, where:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "item 0 → visited",
            "item 1 → not visited",
            "item 2 → visited",
            "item 3 → visited",
            "item 4 → not visited"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Finally,&nbsp;<code>10110</code>&nbsp;in binary equals&nbsp;<strong>22</strong>&nbsp;in decimal, so the mask value is 22.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Now&nbsp;<code>dp[node][22]</code>&nbsp;means \"at this node, having visited items represented by bitmask 22.\" One clean, indexable state.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "3-essential-bitmask-operations",
      "title": "3. Essential Bitmask Operations",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Before writing any bitmask DP, you need to know five operations:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Check if bit i is set",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Use&nbsp;<code>mask &amp; (1 &lt;&lt; i)</code>&nbsp;to check if item i is included.<br>If result ≠ 0 → bit is set (visited), else not.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Example:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197643-531806088.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">For mask = 6 (110), check item 2 →</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<code>6 &amp; (1 &lt;&lt; 2) = 110 &amp; 100 = 100 ≠ 0</code>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "&nbsp;→ item 2 is visited",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Set bit i (mark visited)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Use&nbsp;<code>mask | (1 &lt;&lt; i)</code>&nbsp;to include item i.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Example:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197643-493579306.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Add item 1 to mask = 4 (100) →&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">100 | 010 = 110 = 6</code><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\"></strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Unset bit i (mark not visited)</span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Use&nbsp;<code>mask &amp; ~(1 &lt;&lt; i)</code>&nbsp;to remove item i.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Example:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197644-147504547.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Remove item 2 from mask = 7 (111) →&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">111 &amp; 011 = 011 = 3</code><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Check if all items are visited",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Use&nbsp;<code>mask == (1 &lt;&lt; n) - 1</code>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Example:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197644-576094244.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">For n = 3 →&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">(1 &lt;&lt; 3) - 1 = 111</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;→ all visited</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Count number of visited items",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Use&nbsp;<code>Integer.bitCount(mask)</code>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Example:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197644-212359012.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"><code>Integer.bitCount(6)</code>&nbsp;→&nbsp;<code>110</code>&nbsp;→ 2 items visited",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "<hr style=\"border-width: 1px medium medium; border-style: solid none none; border-color: rgb(224, 224, 224) currentcolor currentcolor; border-image: none; margin: 24px 0px;\">",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "4-when-do-you-use-bitmask-dp",
      "title": "4. When Do You Use Bitmask DP?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>1. Small number of items</strong><br>Used when n is small (usually ≤ 20), since all subsets are explored.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>2. Need to track subsets</strong><br>You must keep track of which items are selected, visited, or used.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>3. Exact combination matters</strong><br>The answer depends on which specific items are chosen, not just how many.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "<p><b>The key signal:</b></p><p>If you think “I need to remember exactly which items I have used so far,” then use Bitmask DP.</p>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "<hr style=\"border-width: 1px medium medium; border-style: solid none none; border-color: rgb(224, 224, 224) currentcolor currentcolor; border-image: none; margin: 24px 0px;\">",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "5-where-bitmask-dp-shows-up",
      "title": "5. Where Bitmask DP Shows Up",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Bitmask DP is useful whenever a problem revolves around handling subsets.&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Classic examples include the&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Traveling Salesman Problem, where the mask represents which cities have been visited,&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Task assignment problems, where it tracks which tasks have already been assigned.&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "It can also appear in shortest path variations where state includes visited nodes, in broken profile DP where the mask represents filled cells of a row, and in set cover problems where the mask tracks which elements have been covered.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The common pattern across all of these is that the mask encodes a subset, and transitions involve adding or removing elements from that subset.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "6-how-to-approach-any-bitmask-dp-problem",
      "title": "6. How to Approach Any Bitmask DP Problem",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The first thing to figure out is what subset you are tracking.&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The entire DP revolves around this decision. It could be visited cities, assigned tasks, or covered elements this becomes your bitmask.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Once that is clear, define the DP state. Typically it looks like&nbsp;<code>dp[mask][...]</code>, where the mask represents the chosen subset and the additional dimension captures context like the current position, last selected item, or any other constraint required to continue the transition.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "After defining the state, think about the base condition. This is usually either the empty subset or a subset with a single element.&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For example, in many problems,&nbsp;<code>dp[0]</code>&nbsp;represents the starting condition, or&nbsp;<code>dp[1 &lt;&lt; i]</code>&nbsp;initializes states with just one element selected.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Then comes the recurrence. From a given mask, you try to extend the subset by adding one more element that is not yet included.&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This usually involves iterating over all possible elements and checking whether their corresponding bit is unset in the mask, then transitioning to a new mask with that element included.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Finally, the answer is typically obtained from the full mask, which represents all elements being included. This is usually&nbsp;<code>(1 &lt;&lt; n) - 1</code>, meaning every bit is set.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
